WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.931
Begin by framing Defining the Computational
Problem as a question about inputs, decisions,

2
00:00:07.931 --> 00:00:09.750
and observable results.

3
00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:17.313
Programming begins with precise problem
statements, input and output examples, and

4
00:00:17.313 --> 00:00:20.750
algorithms written independently of syntax.

5
00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:28.375
Consider this worked case: Given a list of
integers, the problem statement asks to return

6
00:00:28.375 --> 00:00:35.999
the sum of all positive values; inputs are [1,
-2, 3] and outputs are 4.

7
00:00:35.999 --> 00:00:43.194
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

8
00:00:43.194 --> 00:00:47.031
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

9
00:00:47.031 --> 00:00:54.337
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

10
00:00:54.337 --> 00:00:54.859
conclusion.

11
00:00:54.859 --> 00:01:00.453
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

12
00:01:00.578 --> 00:01:07.763
Now define Variables, Expressions, and Control
precisely enough that another learner could

13
00:01:07.763 --> 00:01:09.559
test the definition.

14
00:01:09.559 --> 00:01:16.382
Variables represent values and state,
expressions compute values, and control

15
00:01:16.382 --> 00:01:19.793
structures select or repeat operations.

16
00:01:19.793 --> 00:01:31.010
Consider this worked case: In Python, x = 5 is
a variable assignment; x + 3 is an expression;

17
00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:36.324
if x > 0: print('positive') is a control
structure.

18
00:01:36.324 --> 00:01:43.590
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

19
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:47.465
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

20
00:01:47.465 --> 00:01:53.916
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

21
00:01:53.916 --> 00:01:56.262
evidence would distinguish them.

22
00:01:56.262 --> 00:02:02.918
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

23
00:02:03.026 --> 00:02:10.931
With the foundation in place, examine how
Functions and Decomposition operates in a

24
00:02:10.931 --> 00:02:12.147
concrete sequence.

25
00:02:12.147 --> 00:02:19.819
Functions decompose a solution into named
operations with parameters and results.

26
00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:27.335
Consider this worked case: A learner maps the
inputs, constraints, and observable result for

27
00:02:27.335 --> 00:02:33.241
Functions and Decomposition, then checks each
step against the stated claim.

28
00:02:33.241 --> 00:02:40.293
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

29
00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:44.054
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

30
00:02:44.054 --> 00:02:51.319
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

31
00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:57.132
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

32
00:02:57.242 --> 00:03:04.061
Next, compare the choices involved in
Recursion and Base Cases and make the decision

33
00:03:04.061 --> 00:03:05.035
criteria explicit.

34
00:03:05.035 --> 00:03:11.363
Recursion solves a problem through smaller
instances and requires a base case.

35
00:03:11.363 --> 00:03:18.313
Consider this worked case: A learner maps the
inputs, constraints, and observable result for

36
00:03:18.313 --> 00:03:24.270
Recursion and Base Cases, then checks each
step against the stated claim.

37
00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:31.535
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

38
00:03:31.535 --> 00:03:35.410
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

39
00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:42.481
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

40
00:03:42.481 --> 00:03:43.660
the goalposts.

41
00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:50.613
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

42
00:03:50.738 --> 00:03:58.270
Apply Data Structures: Lists, Stacks, Queues
to a worked situation instead of treating it

43
00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.422
as an isolated definition.

44
00:04:00.422 --> 00:04:08.868
Lists represent ordered sequences, stacks use
last-in first-out access, queues use first-in

45
00:04:08.868 --> 00:04:15.906
first-out access, trees represent hierarchy,
and graphs represent connected entities.

46
00:04:15.906 --> 00:04:24.810
Consider this worked case: A list [1,2,3] can
be pushed onto a stack; popping yields 3, then

47
00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:30.047
2, then 1; a queue would yield 1, 2, 3.

48
00:04:30.047 --> 00:04:37.231
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

49
00:04:37.231 --> 00:04:41.062
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

50
00:04:41.062 --> 00:04:48.533
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

51
00:04:48.533 --> 00:04:49.031
step.

52
00:04:49.031 --> 00:04:53.891
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

53
00:04:54.002 --> 00:05:01.611
Examine the tradeoffs in Trees and Graphs,
including what improves and what becomes more

54
00:05:01.611 --> 00:05:02.155
difficult.

55
00:05:02.155 --> 00:05:06.717
Python objects have identity, a type, and a
value.

56
00:05:06.717 --> 00:05:14.575
Consider this worked case: A family tree is a
tree; a social network where people are nodes

57
00:05:14.575 --> 00:05:17.811
and friendships are edges is a graph.

58
00:05:17.811 --> 00:05:24.883
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

59
00:05:24.883 --> 00:05:28.655
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

60
00:05:28.655 --> 00:05:35.592
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

61
00:05:35.592 --> 00:05:43.045
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

62
00:05:43.154 --> 00:05:50.674
Stress-test the reasoning around Searching and
Sorting with an edge case and identify which

63
00:05:50.674 --> 00:05:52.822
assumption controls the outcome.

64
00:05:52.822 --> 00:05:58.947
Searching and sorting algorithms depend on
representation and invariants.

65
00:05:58.947 --> 00:06:05.399
Consider this worked case: Binary search
requires a sorted list; insertion sort

66
00:06:05.399 --> 00:06:10.775
maintains the invariant that the left portion
is always sorted.

67
00:06:10.775 --> 00:06:18.102
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

68
00:06:18.102 --> 00:06:22.010
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

69
00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:28.369
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

70
00:06:28.369 --> 00:06:35.494
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

71
00:06:35.618 --> 00:06:43.081
Use Correctness, Tests, and Debugging to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

72
00:06:43.081 --> 00:06:45.568
causes, and corrective action.

73
00:06:45.568 --> 00:06:53.114
Correctness arguments, tests, tracing, and
debugging compare intended behavior with

74
00:06:53.114 --> 00:06:58.396
observable results, including boundary and
error cases.

75
00:06:58.396 --> 00:07:06.096
Consider this worked case: Test sum_positive
with empty list, all negatives, and large

76
00:07:06.096 --> 00:07:11.427
numbers; trace variable values to find a
logical error.

77
00:07:11.427 --> 00:07:18.448
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

78
00:07:18.448 --> 00:07:22.192
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

79
00:07:22.192 --> 00:07:29.759
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

80
00:07:29.759 --> 00:07:31.380
smallest plausible cause.

81
00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:39.099
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

82
00:07:39.218 --> 00:07:46.112
Connect Python Language Mechanics to the
earlier ideas and explain how the pieces work

83
00:07:46.112 --> 00:07:47.589
as one system.

84
00:07:47.589 --> 00:07:52.230
Names are bound to objects within namespaces
and scopes.

85
00:07:52.230 --> 00:08:02.547
Consider this worked case: a = [1]; b = a; a.
append(2); print(b) outputs [1,2] because a

86
00:08:02.547 --> 00:08:07.402
and b share identity; try/except handles
division errors.

87
00:08:07.402 --> 00:08:14.678
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

88
00:08:14.678 --> 00:08:18.558
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

89
00:08:18.558 --> 00:08:28.107
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

90
00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:29.699
across representations.

91
00:08:29.699 --> 00:08:37.277
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

92
00:08:37.394 --> 00:08:44.832
Finish with Course Workflow and Communication
as a practical checklist that can guide a new

93
00:08:44.832 --> 00:08:45.328
analysis.

94
00:08:45.328 --> 00:08:51.814
Students will be required to demonstrate oral
and written communication skills through such

95
00:08:51.814 --> 00:08:58.299
assignments as short papers, homeworks, group
discussions and debates, and development of a

96
00:08:58.299 --> 00:08:59.297
term paper.

97
00:08:59.297 --> 00:09:06.102
Consider this worked case: A student writes a
pseudocode solution, translates it to Python,

98
00:09:06.102 --> 00:09:12.906
submits a short paper explaining the approach,
and presents it in a group debate.

99
00:09:12.906 --> 00:09:20.131
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

100
00:09:20.131 --> 00:09:23.984
that the final result satisfies the stated
